% Picture / vector-graphics demonstration for the \picture layer.
%
% Render with:  texish scripts/picture.script -o picture -p letter
%
% Coordinate convention inside a picture: y-up, origin at the bottom-left.
% A bare number is a point; a unit suffix (in, mm, pt, em) is honoured too.
% Coordinates may be computed — a bare variable \x is its value, and arithmetic
% like \*{\x}{14} or \forloop.index works, so plots are built in a \for loop.

\def headline {\italic{Picture demo} \hfil page \the\pageno}
\def footline {\hfil — \the\pageno — \hfil}

\font lmroman 12 regular

\bold{\TeXish{} pictures — vector graphics in the document}

A \italic{picture} is a fixed-size box you draw into with shapes, paths, and
placed type. It flows in the text like any other box, so a diagram sits beside
prose, scales with the surrounding units, and prints through the same Cairo or
Graphics2D backend as the page itself.

\bold{Shapes and colour}

Each shape draws immediately using the current fill and stroke colour. Set the
pen with \italic{\\stroke}, the fill with \italic{\\fill}, and turn either off
with \italic{\\nostroke} / \italic{\\nofill}.

\picture width:4in height:1.6in {
  \fill{lightsteelblue} \stroke{steelblue} \linewidth{1.5pt}
  \rect{0.2in 0.2in 1in 1in}
  \circle{2in 0.7in 0.5in}
  \nofill \stroke{firebrick}
  \ellipse{3.2in 0.7in 0.6in 0.4in}
  \fill{seagreen} \nostroke
  \polygon{0.2in 1.4in  0.7in 1.4in  0.45in 0.2in}
}

\bold{Line thickness and line type}

Stroke style is graphics state: line width in any unit, and a line type that is
either a named preset or a raw dash array. Round caps render a dotted line as
true dots.

\picture width:4in height:1.4in {
  \stroke{black}
  \linewidth{0.5pt} \line{0.2in 1.2in  3.8in 1.2in}
  \linewidth{2pt}   \line{0.2in 0.95in 3.8in 0.95in}
  \linewidth{4pt}   \line{0.2in 0.7in  3.8in 0.7in}
  \linewidth{1.5pt} \linetype{dashed} \line{0.2in 0.45in 3.8in 0.45in}
  \linetype{dotted} \linecap{round}   \line{0.2in 0.2in  3.8in 0.2in}
}

\bold{Paths and curves}

A \italic{\\path} builds a freeform outline from move/line/curve/arc segments
and draws it with the current state. Here a filled Bézier wave with a stroked
edge.

\picture width:4in height:1.4in {
  \fill{papayawhip} \stroke{chocolate} \linewidth{1.5pt}
  \path{
    \moveto{0.2in 0.2in}
    \lineto{0.2in 0.8in}
    \curveto{1.2in 1.6in  2.6in -0.4in  3.8in 0.8in}
    \lineto{3.8in 0.2in}
    \close
  }
}

\bold{Transforms and groups}

\italic{\\translate}, \italic{\\scale}, and \italic{\\rotate} compose on the
coordinate system; \italic{\\group} saves the state so changes inside it are
undone after. Rotating one square in a loop draws a fan.

\picture width:3in height:3in {
  \translate{1.5in 1.5in}
  \nofill \stroke{mediumpurple} \linewidth{1pt}
  \for\i{\range{1}{12}}{
    \group{
      \rotate{\*{\i}{30}}
      \rect{0 0 1.2in 0.12in}
    }
  }
}

\bold{Placing type and glyphs}

\italic{\\at} drops fully typeset content — text or math — at a coordinate with
an anchor; \italic{\\glyph} places a single glyph for a marker. Relative
coordinates (\italic{++}) are measured from the current point.

\picture width:4in height:1.6in {
  \stroke{gray} \linewidth{1pt}
  \line{0.3in 0.3in  3.7in 1.3in}
  \fill{crimson} \glyph anchor:center {0.3in 0.3in}{8226}
  \fill{crimson} \glyph anchor:center {3.7in 1.3in}{8226}
  \at anchor:south {2in 0.8in}{$E = mc^2$}
  \at anchor:west {0.3in 0.3in}{start}
  \at anchor:east {3.7in 1.3in}{end}
}

\eject

\bold{Application — a line graph}

The texish language already has loops and arithmetic, so a plot is just a path
built in a \italic{\\for}. Axes are lines; the curve samples $y = x^2$.

\picture width:3in height:2.4in {
  \stroke{gray} \linewidth{1pt}
  \line{0 0  2.8in 0}
  \line{0 0  0 2.2in}
  \stroke{royalblue} \linewidth{1.5pt}
  \path{
    \moveto{0 0}
    \for\i{\range{1}{14}}{
      \lineto{\*{\i}{14}  \*{\*{\i}{\i}}{0.8}}
    }
  }
  \at anchor:south {1.4in -0.1in}{$x$}
  \at anchor:west {0.05in 2.1in}{$y = x^2$}
}

\bold{Application — a bar chart}

A data sequence drives the bars; \italic{\\forloop.index} positions each one.

\set data {\seq{60 110 80 140 95 120}}

\picture width:3in height:2.4in {
  \stroke{gray} \linewidth{1pt} \line{0 0  2.8in 0}
  \fill{teal} \nostroke
  \for\v{\data}{
    \rect{\*{\forloop.index}{42} 0 30 \v}
  }
}

\bold{Application — a molecule}

A skeletal benzene ring: a hexagon of bonds, inner lines for the aromatic
double bonds, and atom labels placed with \italic{\\at}.

\picture width:2.4in height:2in {
  \nofill \stroke{black} \linewidth{1.5pt}
  \polygon{108 72  90 103  54 103  36 72  54 41  90 41}
  \linewidth{1pt}
  \line{98 74  86 93}
  \line{58 93  46 74}
  \line{60 49  84 49}
  \linewidth{1.5pt}
  \line{108 72  128 72}
  \at anchor:west {132 72}{OH}
}

This page is set entirely by \TeXish: the prose, the running heads, and every
figure above share one rendering pipeline.
